GEUL Watchdog Timer:
	In GEUL, Watchdog timer is used to restart the system incase hang of L1C tasks, secondary E200 cores, VSPAs.

	The Watchdog timer is a 32-bit down counter that notifies the system if the counter has not been reset by software in a specified time.
	There will be two watchdog timeouts: in the 1st timeout generates Watchdog Interrupt (WDOGINT). 
        Watchdog Interrupt (Active High) is connected to the MPIC Internal Interrupt 21(WDOGINT).
	After the 1st timeout, the counter value is reloaded and down counting continues, 
	if the counter reaches zero again before the interrupt is cleared, 
	then 2nd time out will occur. In this case, the watchdog hardware will raise Reset notification to Host(LS1046).

	Upon Host(or LS1046) receives the interrupt(Reset notification), it notifies the MAC about Hung condition. 
	MAC do the Clean up procedures and MAC request the reset of MODEM to Host.
	Modem’s HRESET is connected to a GPIO on LS1046, this GPIO is pulled to reset the Modem.

	In Geul, Watchdog timer is started by L1C and once the timer is started its countdown starts
	and after 1st timeout, Geul watchdog interrupt handler executes.
	Watchdog Interrupt handler calls to L1C for getting the components (L1C tasks, secondary E200 cores, VSPAs) functioning status. 
	If all the components are found to be working the watchdog counter is reloaded again and Modem continues working as usual.
	If either one or more components are found not working or the ISR itself didn’t execute, 
	the watchdog hardware will raise Reset notification to IO pin on Geul SOC which cause interrupt on Host.


GEUL Watchdog API:
Application can we use the following GEUL Watchdog APIs.
The Watchdog API(watchdog_api.h) is in "geul_freertos_la12xx_devel/FreeRTOS/Demo/E200_MPC57XXX_GEUL_GCC/include/" directory.

   1.vWdogStart
     Application can use this API to Initialize and then Start/Enable Watchdog.
     
     Syntax:
     void vWdogStart( uint32_t  wdog_load_val,  int (*L1C_callback_func)(void) );

     @params:
	wdog_load_val - Watchdog load value  
	L1C_callback_func -  L1C code Call back function, this callback function should pass by L1C.

      @return:
	void

   2.vWdogStop
     Application can use this API to Stop/Disable Watchdog.
     Use vWdogStart to start WDOG again if used vWdogStop.

     Syntax:
     void vWdogStop(void);

     @params:
	void
     @return:
	void

    3.vWdogReload
      Application can use this API to Reload Watchdog timer at run time.

      Syntax:
      void vWdogReload(uint32_t  new_wdog_load_val,  int (*L1C_callback_func)(void));

      @params:
	new_wdog_load_val: new Watchdog load value
	L1C_callback_func: L1C code Call back function, this callback function can be same which passed to “vWdogStart” function.


Watchdog interrupt handler:-
    wdogIRQHandler:
    This function executes every time once the 1st timeout occurs.
    It checks L1C components status by calling the L1C call back function(which passed in vWdogStart or vWdogReload function).
    If the return value of call back function is 1 then reload the watchdog timer, else if the return value is 0,
    then don't load timer, so after 2nd timeout watchdog hardware send the reset notification to Host(LS1046).

    bool_t wdogIRQHandler(uint32_t ulIrq_No, void * vDev_Data);
	ulIrq_No - Interrupt number, which is MPIC watchdog interrupt
        vDev_Data - Any private device info, here NULL is passed


GEUL WatchdogTest:-
    vWatchdogTest:
    This test function is only for internal tetsing.
    It starts the watchdog timer by loading the timer value and also provides the callbcak function for getting status.
    The watchdog load value is calculted using following:
	A counter value 1 corresponds to 30.5us , 
	So Ideally the Decimal equivalent of [Value loaded in Hex in WDOG] x 30.5 would give the time in microseconds


